"[2510.11238v1] Attacks by Content: Automated Fact-checking is an AI Security Issue (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11238v1)\nciteturn2701view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 10 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2700view3\",\"lineno\":40}); Total lines: 200\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:  cite55†Image: Cornell University L3: \nL4:  We gratefully acknowledge support from the Simons Foundation, cite1†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , and all contributors. cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org L5: \nL6: cite56†Image: arxiv logo > cite3†cs > arXiv:2510.11238v1\nL7: \nL8: cite4†Help†info.arxiv.org | cite5†Advanced Search L9: \nL10:  All fields Title Author Abstract Comments Journal reference ACM classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text\nL11: \nL12: Search\nL13: cite57†Image: arXiv logo L14: \nL15: cite6† †www.cornell.edu L16: \nL17:  open search\nL18: \nL19: GO\nL20: \nL21: open navigation menu\nL22: ## quick links\nL23: \nL24:   * cite7†Login L25:   * cite4†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L26:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL29: \nL30: arXiv:2510.11238v1 (cs)\nL31: \nL32: [Submitted on 13 Oct 2025]\nL33: # Title:Attacks by Content: Automated Fact-checking is an AI Security Issue\nL34: \nL35: Authors:cite9†Michael Schlichtkrull L36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Attacks by Content: Automated Fact-checking is an AI Security Issue, by Michael Schlichtkrull\nL38: \nL39: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L40: > Abstract:When AI agents retrieve and reason over external documents, adversaries can manipulate the data they receive to subvert their behaviour. Previous research has studied indirect prompt injection, where the attacker injects malicious instructions. We argue that injection of instructions is not necessary to manipulate agents - attackers could instead supply biased, misleading, or false information. We term this an attack by content.\nL41: Existing defenses, which focus on detecting hidden commands, are ineffective against attacks by content. To defend themselves and their users, agents must critically evaluate retrieved information, corroborating claims with external evidence and evaluating source trustworthiness. We argue that this is analogous to an existing NLP task, automated fact-checking, which we propose to repurpose as a cognitive self-defense tool for agents.\nL42: Comments: Accepted to EMNLP 2025\nL43: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL44: Cite as: cite12†arXiv:2510.11238 [cs.CL]\nL45:   (or  cite13†arXiv:2510.11238v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL46:   cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.11238†doi.org L47: \nL48: Focus to learn more\nL49: \nL50:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL51: ## Submission history\nL52: \nL53: From: Michael Sejr Schlichtkrull [cite15†view email ]\nL54: [v1] Mon, 13 Oct 2025 10:18:48 UTC (9,453 KB)\nL55: \nL56: Full-text links:\nL57: ## Access Paper:\nL58: \nL59:  View a PDF of the paper titled Attacks by Content: Automated Fact-checking is an AI Security Issue, by Michael Schlichtkrull\nL60: \nL61:   * cite10†View PDF L62:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L63:   * cite16†TeX Source  L64: \nL65: cite17†view license L66: \nL67: Current browse context:\nL68: \nL69: cs.CL\nL70: \nL71: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L72: \nL73: cite20†new |  cite21†recent | cite22†2025-10 L74: \nL75: Change to browse by:\nL76: \nL77: cite23†cs L78: cite24†cs.AI L79: ### References & Citations\nL80: \nL81:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L82:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L83:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L84: \nL85: export BibTeX citation Loading...\nL86: \nL87: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL88: \nL89: ×\nL90: \nL91: loading...\nL92: \nL93: Data provided by:\nL94: \nL95: ### Bookmark\nL96: \nL97: cite28† †www.bibsonomy.org cite29† †reddit.com L98: \nL99: Bibliographic Tools\nL100: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL101: \nL102: Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL103: \nL104: Bibliographic Explorer (cite30†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL105: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.11288v1] Emergent Misalignment via In-Context Learning: Narrow in-context examples can produce broadly misaligned LLMs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11288v1)\nciteturn2701view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2700view4\",\"lineno\":14}); Total lines: 159\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 20 Apr 2026 (cite5†v4 )]\nL9: # Title:Emergent Misalignment via In-Context Learning: Narrow in-context examples can produce broadly misaligned LLMs\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Nikita Afonin , cite7†Nikita Andriyanov , cite8†Nikhil Bageshpura , cite9†Kyle Liu , cite10†Kevin Zhu , cite11†Sunishchal Dev , cite12†Ashwinee Panda , cite13†Alexander Panchenko , cite14†Oleg Rogov , cite15†Elena Tutubalina , cite16†Mikhail Seleznyov L12: View a PDF of the paper titled Emergent Misalignment via In-Context Learning: Narrow in-context examples can produce broadly misaligned LLMs, by Nikita Afonin and 10 other authors\nL13: \nL14: cite17†View PDF cite18†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:Recent work has shown that narrow finetuning can produce broadly misaligned LLMs, a phenomenon termed emergent misalignment (EM). While concerning, these findings were limited to finetuning and activation steering, leaving out in-context learning (ICL). We therefore ask: does EM emerge in ICL? We find that it does: across three datasets, three frontier models produce broadly misaligned responses at rates between 2% and 17% given 64 narrow in-context examples, and up to 58% with 256 examples.\nL16: We also examine mechanisms of EM by eliciting step-by-step reasoning (while leaving in-context examples unchanged). Manual analysis of the resulting chain-of-thought shows that 67.5% of misaligned traces explicitly rationalize harmful outputs by adopting a reckless or dangerous ''persona'', echoing prior results on finetuning-induced EM.\nL17: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL18: Cite as:  | cite19†arXiv:2510.11288 [cs.CL]\nL19:    | (or cite20†arXiv:2510.11288v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL20:    | cite21†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.11288†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL21: ## Submission history\nL22: \nL23: From: Mikhail Seleznyov [cite22†view email ]\nL24: [v1] Mon, 13 Oct 2025 11:23:56 UTC (421 KB)\nL25: cite23†[v2] Fri, 16 Jan 2026 09:39:36 UTC (148 KB)\nL26: cite24†[v3] Mon, 19 Jan 2026 07:02:10 UTC (148 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v4] Mon, 20 Apr 2026 09:09:30 UTC (150 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Emergent Misalignment via In-Context Learning: Narrow in-context examples can produce broadly misaligned LLMs, by Nikita Afonin and 10 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite17†View PDF L35:   * cite18†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite25†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite26†view license L39: \nL40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite27†< prev |   cite28†next > L45: \nL46: cite29†new | cite30†recent | cite31†2025-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite32†cs L51: ### References & Citations\nL52: \nL53:   * cite33†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L54:   * cite34†Google Scholar†scho