cite24†5.2 Evaluation Metrics L35:     3. cite25†5.3 Baseline vs. Guarded Pipelines with DeepResearchGuard L36:       1. cite26†DeepResearchGuard substantially boosts DSR across models while keeping ORR low. L37:       2. cite27†Integrating DeepResearchGuard consistently lifts report quality across all models. L38: cite28†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L39: \nL40: arXiv:2510.10994v1 [cs.CL] 13 Oct 2025\nL41: # DeepResearchGuard: Deep Research with Open-Domain Evaluation and Multi-Stage Guardrails for Safety\nL42: Wei-Chieh Huang Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Henry Peng Zou Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Yaozu Wu Affiliation: University of Tokyo    Dongyuan Li Affiliation: University of Tokyo    Yankai Chen Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Weizhi Zhang Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Yangning Li Affiliation: Tsinghua University    Angelo Zangari Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Jizhou Guo Affiliation: Shanghai Jiao Tong University{whuang80, ychen588, psyu}@uic.edu, jiangrh@csis.u-tokyo.ac.jp    Chunyu Miao Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Liancheng Fang Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Langzhou He Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Renhe Jiang Affiliation: University of Tokyo    Philip S.\nL43: Yu Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago\nL44: ###### Abstract\nL45: Deep research frameworks have shown promising capabilities in synthesizing comprehensive reports from web sources. While deep research possesses significant potential to address complex issues through planning and research cycles, existing frameworks are deficient in sufficient evaluation procedures and stage-specific protections.\nL89: In contrast, deep research unfolds over multiple stages (input, planning, research, report) and introduces distinct risks (xu2025comprehensive), such as plan drift and web-retrieval threats that bypass single-turn moderation. Hence, the guardrails designed for LLMs and single-turn agents are insufficient for deep research systems; they require stage-aware, interlocking safeguards that validate input, plans, vet sources, and verify report outputs across the entire pipeline.\nL90: ## 3 DeepResearchGuard Framework\nL91: ### 3.1 Taxonomy for Stages In DeepResearchGuard\nL92: The taxonomy and rules in DeepResearchGuard start from an initial examination, integration, and consolidation of prior research on input-output taxonomies for LLM Agents (DBLP:conf/iclr/YinLPZMVRAXW25; DBLP:conf/nips/HanREJL00D24; DBLP:conf/acl/WangZYWMGWW25). We review and adapt the most relevant aspects for deep research.\nL93: Furthermore, we formulate objective standards and stage-specific taxonomies for the planning, research, and output stages, establishing a unified framework design for deep research tasks. In our taxonomy (see Appendix ), we design the severity $s$ to quantify the harmful level of the content in each stage, as represented in Figure cite31†1 . If $s=3$, DeepResearchGuard terminates the process to prevent harmful content from propagating.\nL94: If $s\\in\\{1,2\\}$, DeepResearchGuard revises problematic content and passes the revised content to the subsequent stage.\nL95: ### 3.2 General Guard Rule for Agent in each Stage\nL96: DeepResearchGuard applies multiple guard agents in the deep research workflow at input, planning, research, and output stages, evaluates content with memory, and escalates to human review when confidence is low. Each guard agent classifies content, assigns a severity, and edits or rejects it. Let $k\\in\\{\\text{input},\\text{plan},\\text{output}\\}$ index the Input, Plan, and Output guards, respectively.\nL97: Each guard $G_{k}$ receives the upstream message $m_{k}$, the contextual state $C$, and the long-term memory $M$. It predicts a category $y_{k}\\in\\mathcal{Y}$ and a severity $s_{k}\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, then chooses an action $a_{k}$ and produces a revised message $m_{k}^{\\prime}$, if required:\nL98:  | $\\displaystyle(y_{k},s_{k})$  | $\\displaystyle=f_{k}(m_{k},C,M),$  |  | (1)\nL99:  | $\\displaystyle(a_{k},m_{k}^{\\prime})$  | $\\displaystyle=\\pi_{k}(y_{k},s_{k},m_{k}).$  |  | (2)\nL100: \nL101: Here, $f_{k}$ is the stage-$k$ LLM classifier and $\\pi_{k}$ is the stage-specific policy. If $s_{k}=3$, the pipeline gets hard refusal by DeepResearchGuard; if $s_{k}\\in\\{1,2\\}$, the policy either redacts and resumes or repairs and runs.\nL102: ### 3.3 Memory Retrieval and Planning of Guard Agent\nL103: We cache processed cases for retrieval to support evaluation. As shown in Figure cite31†1 , the first step of each guard agent is to look up a similar case in the memory.\nL104: Let $k\\in\\{\\text{input},\\text{plan},\\text{research},\\text{output}\\}$ denote the guard stage; $p$ the current query prompt; $\\{p_{j}\\}$ prior contents $j$ in long-term memory; $y_{j}\\in\\mathcal{Y}$ the stored category label for item $j$; $\\tau_{a,j}\\in[0,1]$ the stored classifier confidence; $s(p,p_{j})\\in[0,1]$ a semantic similarity; $\\tau_{\\text{sim}}$ the similarity threshold; and $L\\in\\mathbb{N}$ the number of top matches kept.\nL105: The process of finding similar cases in long-term memory and forming the prompt for the guard is described in Algorithm cite32†1 . In addition, the planning variable $\\mathit{approach}\\in\\{\\textsc{standard},\\textsc{cautious},\\textsc{conservative}\\}$ controls the human intervention threshold $\\tau_{h}$ and the reasoning budget $C_{r}$ (e.g., $\\tau_{h}{=}0.5/0.7/0.8$ with $C_{r}{=}\\textsc{medium}/\\textsc{high}$).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDUAL-Bench: Measuring Over-Refusal and Robustnessin Vision-Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10846v1)\nciteturn2701view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2700view1\",\"pattern\":\"Methodology\"}); Total lines