:   12. cite38†A Appendix L49:     1. cite39†A.1 Related Work L50:     2. cite40†A.2 Problem Formulation - Ideal Response behavior L51:     3. cite41†A.3 Problem Formulation - Label Space L52:       1. cite42†Unified Label Space L53:     4. cite43†A.4 Or-bench LLM as Judge PROMPT L54:     5. cite44†A.5 Safe Completion LLM as Judge PROMPT L55:     6. cite45†A.6 Metrics Explanation L56:   13. cite46†B Data Construction Pipeline L57:     1. cite47†B.0.1 AIM PROMPT L58:     2. cite48†B.0.2 13 Categories Definition L59:     3. cite49†B.0.3 Harm seed PROMPT L60:     4. cite50†B.0.4 Detoxification Prompt L61:     5. cite51†B.1 Semantics-Preserving Perturbations L62:     6. cite52†B.2 Annotation and Quality Control L63:   14. cite53†C Evaluation Protocol L64:     1. cite54†C.1 Evaluation Item Schema L65:     2. cite55†C.2 Judging Procedure L66:     3. cite56†C.3 Quality Control and Reliability L67:       1. cite57†Multi-layer verification. L68:       2. cite58†Evaluation environment and reproducibility. L69:   15. cite59†D Experiment setup L70:     1. cite60†Dataset & Benchmark Design. L71:     2. cite61†Evaluation Protocol. L72:     3. cite62†Implementation Details. L73:     4. cite63†OCR Baseline L74:     5. cite64†Human Baseline L75:   16. cite65†E Case Study L76:   17. cite66†F More Results L77:     1. cite67†F.1 Category-wise Results L78:     2. cite68†F.2 Ablation Study L169: ### 3.3 Label Space & Judgment Criteria\nL170: \nL171: In our task setup, input instances consist of both harmful images $I_{\\text{harm}}$ and safe images $I_{\\text{safe}}$. Since the task is open-ended generation, there is no single reference answer; instead, we evaluate model behavior by categorizing outputs into a unified label space.\nL172: Specifically, we adopt two complementary label systems: Direct Answer (da) and Refusal (r) from (cite73†Cui et al., 2025 ), and Safe Completion (sc) and Non Safe Completion (non-sc) from cite72†Yuan et al. (2025) . The detailed definitions and annotation criteria for each label are provided in Appendix cite41†A.3 .\nL173: ##### Judgment Methodology\nL174: \nL175: Our judgment protocol builds upon the LLM-as-a-Judge framework proposed in OR-Bench (cite73†Cui et al., 2025 )^{3}^{3} 3 OR-bench LLM as Judge prompt can be found in Appendix cite43†A.4 . Specifically, model outputs are categorized into three labels: Direct Answer, Direct Refuse, and Indirect Refuse, and same as Or-Bench we then group Direct Refuse and Indirect Refuse together as label Refusal.\nL176: In addition, inspired by the human-annotated criteria in (cite72†Yuan et al., 2025 ), where Safe Completion is defined as both safe and helpful, we extend this idea by employing an automatic LLM-as-a-Judge pipeline tailored to our fixed instruction task (“Describe the image”), which enables the classification of Safe Completion^{4}^{4} 4 Safe Completion LLM as Judge prompt can be found in Appendix cite44†A.5 .\nL177: ### 3.4 Metrics\nL178: \nL179: Building on our categorization scheme, we evaluate model behavior using Refusal Rate (RR) and Direct Answer Rate (DAR) metrics from OR-Bench (cite73†Cui et al., 2025 ), Instruction Robustness Gap ($\\Delta IR$) from (cite82†Maskey et al., 2025 ) and Safe Completion Rate (SCR) from (cite72†Yuan et al., 2025 ) ^{5}^{5} 5 For detailed definitions and analysis of the metric fluctuations, please refer to Appendix cite45†A.6 ..\nL180: ##### Refusal Rate (RR).\nL181: \nL182: We compute the Refusal Rate separately for harmful and benign instructions as the proportion of instances in which the model issues a direct refusal:\nL183: \nL184:  | $$\\text{RR}_{c}=\\frac{1}{|I_{c}|}\\sum_{I\\in I_{c}}\\mathbf{1}[R\\in\\text{r}],c\\in\\{\\text{h},\\text{b}\\}.$$  |\nL185: ##### Direct Answer Rate (DAR)\nL186: \nL187: We compute the Direct Answer Rate separately for harmful and benign instructions as the proportion of instances in which the model issues a direct answer:\nL188: \nL189:  | $$\\text{DAR}_{c}=\\frac{1}{|I_{c}|}\\sum_{I\\in I_{c}}\\mathbf{1}[R\\in\\text{da}],c\\in\\{\\text{h},\\text{b}\\}.$$  |\nL190: ##### Instruction Robustness Gap ($\\Delta IR$).\nL191: \nL192: Following (cite82†Maskey et al., 2025 ), we define the difference in a model’s ability to follow instructions as\nL193: \nL194:  | $$\\Delta\\text{IR}=\\text{DAR}_{\\text{b}}-\\text{DAR}_{\\text{h}}.$$  |\nL195: ##### Safe Completion Rate (SCR).\nL196: \nL197: Following the “safety + helpfulness” notion of safe completion (cite72†Yuan et al., 2025 ), we compute the share of harmful-image cases where the model both warns about potential risks and completes the benign portion of the request:\nL198: \nL199:  | $$\\text{SCR}=\\frac{1}{|I_{\\text{h}}|}\\sum_{I\\in I_{\\text{h}}}\\mathbf{1}[R\\in\\text{sc}].$$  |\nL200: \nL201: ### 3.5 Robustness & Stability under Perturbations\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnlocking LLM Safeguards for Low-Resource Languages via Reasoning and Alignment with Minimal Training Data (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10677v1)\nciteturn2701view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2700view2\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 280\nL138: Language  | en  | fr  | zh-cn  | jp  | bn  | hi\nL139: OpenAI Moderation\nL140: w/o. Alignment  | 77.40  | 77.67  | 77.45  | 76.40  | 71.15  | 71.98\nL141: w/ DPO Alignment  | 78.48$\\uparrow$  | 77.52  | 72.14  | 76.28  | 71.10  | 70.82\nL142: w/ CAO Alignment  | 78.94$\\uparrow$  | 76.46  | 76.83  | 77.50$\\uparrow$  | 72.10$\\uparrow$  | 73.26$\\uparrow$\nL143: ToxicChat\nL144: w/o. Alignment  | 84.85  | 83.23  | 81.42  | 80.59  | 72.92  | 73.66\nL145: w/ DPO Alignment  | 83.80  | 81.76  | 73.57  | 82.64$\\uparrow$  | 71.75  | 72.45\nL146: w/ CAO Alignment  | 84.26  | 82.39  | 82.32$\\uparrow$  | 81.22$\\uparrow$  | 73.55$\\uparrow$  | 73.79$\\uparrow$\nL147: Results show that the proposed CAO brings performance rises to most languages while DPO fails. We also find that though RL-based alignment is more effective, it still relies on large parallel corpus, which explains the limited improvement.\nL148: ## 4 Conclusion\nL149: This work presents a multi-stage training framework combining distillation, reinforcement, and alignment to tackle performance insufficiency and imbalance in multilingual safeguard task. Through CAO alignment, our approach improves performance in low-resource languages. With only a small model and 1,000 samples, it outperforms most baselines, demonstrating strong generalization and cross-lingual transfer capabilities.\nL150: Our findings highlight the importance of controllable reasoning chains and alignment for effective multilingual knowledge transfer.\nL151: ## Limitations\nL152: Despite promising results, our work has several limitations. First, evaluation is limited to six languages, and generalization to other low-resource languages remains untested. Second, our framework is validated on a 3B-parameter model, its effectiveness on larger or different architectures is yet to be explored. Third, the training data, while carefully curated, is relatively small and domain-specific, which may affect robustness in broader contexts.\nL153: Moreover, our evaluation focuses primarily on classification accuracy, and more comprehensive assessments, such as human preference or long-context evaluations are needed. Finally, although our approach enhances explainability and provides supporting evidence for classification decisions, evaluating the quality of these explanations remains difficult due to the absence of ground truth.\nL154: ## Ethics Statement\nL155: \nL156: The datasets and large language models used in our study come from open-access repositories. This ensures that we comply with all relevant ethical standards and authorizations. We strictly follow established research ethics throughout our research.\nL157: \nL158: ## Acknowledgement\nL159: \nL160: Our work is supported by Research Fund of National Language Commission (No. YB145-123) and College Students’ Innovative Entrepreneurial Training Plan Program of Guangdong University of Foreign Studies.\nL161: ## References\nL162:   * Chai et al. (2025) Linzheng Chai, Jian Yang, Tao Sun, Hongcheng Guo, Jiaheng Liu, Bing Wang, Xinnian Liang, Jiaqi Bai, Tongliang Li, Qiyao Peng, and Zhoujun Li. 2025. cite45†Xcot: Cross-lingual instruction tuning for cross-lingual chain-of-thought reasoning†doi.org . Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, 39(22):23550–23558.\nL163:   * Chen et al. (2025a) Xingyu Chen, Jiahao Xu, Tian Liang, Zhiwei He, Jianhui Pang, Dian Yu, Linfeng Song, Qiuzhi Liu, Mengfei Zhou, Zhuosheng Zhang, Rui Wang, Zhaopeng Tu, Haitao Mi, and Dong Yu. 2025a. cite46†Do not think that much for 2+3=? on the overthinking of o1-like llms . Preprint, arXiv:2412.21187.\nL164:   * Chen et al. (2025b) Zhuowei Chen, Qiannan Zhang, and Shichao Pei. 2025b. cite47†Injecting universal jailbreak backdoors into LLMs in minutes†openreview.net . In The Thirteenth International Conference on Learning Representations.\n"
